<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Keren</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-african-campaign-world-war-ii">East African campaign (World War II)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keren">Keren</a> was fought from February to March 1941 in the mountains around the town of Keren in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, between Italian forces and British and Indian troops. After weeks of costly attacks on the strong Italian positions, the British broke through on 27 March. The victory led to the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asmara">Asmara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a> and the end of Italian rule in Eritrea.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-keren.jpg" alt="Battle of Keren" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Francis (Frank) Hurley · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_troops_stand_next_to_a_Cheren_(Keren)_signpost,_May_1941.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the Italian declaration of war on 10 June 1940, Italian forces from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> took Kassala and other border posts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>. The British built up more than two divisions in Sudan under Lieutenant-General William Platt, which invaded Eritrea through Kassala on 18 January 1941 and by 1 February had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agordat">Agordat</a>. Keren was not fortified, but steep granite mountains and ridges surrounded it. The road and railway to the town ran through the narrow Dongolaas Gorge, overlooked by Fort Dologorodoc on one side and Mount Sanchil on the other, and the Italians had blocked the gorge by blowing down its crags.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The 11th Indian Infantry Brigade attacked on 5 February. The Cameron Highlanders took the ridge in front of Sanchil, later called Cameron Ridge, and held it under constant fire, but the Savoia Grenadiers drove the Indians back from Brig's Peak and Sanchil. An attack on Acqua Col on 12 February also failed, and Richhpal Ram was awarded a posthumous Victoria Cross for his part in it. Platt paused to regroup and train, while Briggsforce, including two Free French battalions, broke through the Mescelit pass north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keren">Keren</a> on 1 March.</p><p>By March some 25,000 defenders faced more than 13,000 attackers. On 15 March the 4th Indian Division attacked towards Sanchil without success, while the 5th Indian Division took Pinnacle that night and captured Fort Dologorodoc on the morning of 16 March, holding it for ten days against Italian counter-attacks. Major-General Lewis Heath then judged that the key to the gorge lay in two small features overlooking the roadblock. On 25 March troops advancing from the railway tunnel took them by surprise, the engineers reopened the road by midday on 26 March, and on 27 March the Italians withdrew towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asmara">Asmara</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Estimates of the losses differ. The 4th Indian Division's history of 1948 gave 3,273 British casualties, and Raugh in 1993 wrote of 536 British killed and 3,229 wounded and 3,000 Italians killed. With the route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asmara">Asmara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a> open, the 4th Indian Division was sent back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. At Massawa Rear-Admiral Mario Bonetti surrendered in April, and 9,590 prisoners were taken; scuttled ships blocked the harbour, but the British had the port in use by 1 May. On 11 April President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a> lifted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>'s status as a combat zone, opening it to US merchant ships.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Keren” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>British forces took it in 1941 after the <b>Battle of Keren</b>, beginning the British Administration of Eritrea.</blockquote><a href="/state/italian-eritrea">Italian Eritrea</a></li></ul></section>
